**Definition 5.3 (physical entry and exit predicates).** Let
$\mathcal E$ be the finite set of actual version/PC/row-effect pairs for
the outer acquired-choice01 dispatch CTL whose successor is the first
header-Base/setup label of $F^A$. Let $\mathcal X$ be the finite set for
$F^A$'s final source-idle CTL, after row ACK and source-token TOK, whose
successor is InnerReturn. These are the separately numbered inlined
sites of ARC Definitions 4.1 and 5.2, with their two parity alternatives
in $G_{\rm sel}$. They are not a test for any equal numeral occurring
inside a word or a nested simulation. Resolve their numeric pairs from
Definition 2.3's actual captured SourceEntrySet/SourceExitSet slots,
validate them there, and retain the two finite lists as bank data.
Comparisons traverse those actual lists with Equal. The graph contains
fixed slot indices and symbolic control edges, not a literal embedding
of its own generated numeric labels. Neither list contains an absolute
tally or an endpoint table for future covers.

An entry event for item $j$ has its represented physical pre-row control
in $\mathcal E$, its exact installed successor, and completed-cycle
annotation $j$. An exit event has its physical control in $\mathcal X$,
its InnerReturn successor, and the same annotation $j$. If the entry
event is $X_q\to X_{q+1}$ define $e=q+1$; if the exit is
$X_v\to X_{v+1}$ define $f=v+1$. Thus $[e,f]$ includes every cut of
the actual $F^A$ call from its first header prelude through its final
idle CTL, but not the earlier gate or later normalization/CaptureCAP.
Those surrounding cuts are still covered by AcquisitionOffset.

